A device loss must not abort the process from a staging buffer's destructor.

Found by VR phase 1a's gate (lane VR-1A, 2026-09-16), in a suite that has nothing
to do with VR: `scripting.e2e.physics` died "Subprocess aborted" with a
kernel-confirmed `NVRM: Xid 109 CTX SWITCH TIMEOUT` naming that app's own pid. The
crash backtrace (evidence: spikes/openxr-vulkan/xid-physics-crash.log) is

    Root::_fireFrameStarted
      -> VulkanRenderSystem::validateDevice
      -> VulkanRenderSystem::handleDeviceLost
      -> VulkanVaoManager::destroyVkResources
      -> VaoManager::deleteStagingBuffers
      -> VulkanStagingBuffer::~VulkanStagingBuffer
      -> VulkanStagingBuffer::wait( VkFence )
      -> checkVkResult( VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST ) THROWS
      -> __cxa_call_terminate -> SIGABRT

THE MECHANISM, and why it is not bad luck. `~VulkanStagingBuffer` waits on its last
fence unconditionally (OgreVulkanStagingBuffer.cpp:58-59) and `wait()` ends in
`checkVkResult`, which raises on a bad VkResult (:142-150). A destructor is
implicitly `noexcept` since C++11, so a throw leaving it is `std::terminate`. And
`handleDeviceLost()` - the ONLY recovery path this render system has - destroys
every staging buffer on its way through `destroyVkResources`. So at this pin EVERY
device loss that reaches the recovery path aborts the process, by construction: the
recovery code cannot run far enough to recover, and the caller never even gets the
exception it was supposed to get.

THE FIX is one guard, in the destructor: skip the wait when the device is already
lost. A fence on a lost device can never be signalled, so the wait is not merely
dangerous, it is meaningless; `vkDestroyFence` (via `deleteFences`) and the pool
return below it are both legal on a lost device, so nothing else in the destructor
needs to change. The device is reached exactly as `wait()` reaches it - through the
VaoManager - and the manager pointer the destructor already computes is simply
hoisted above the wait. A skipped wait logs one LML_CRITICAL line, so a device loss
still says so in the log instead of passing silently.

SCOPE: the read walked the whole of `handleDeviceLost`'s teardown and this is the
only throwing wait on it (`VulkanQueue::stallIgnoringDeviceLost` already exists for
exactly this reason and is what the rest of that path uses). Nothing outside a lost
device changes: on a healthy device the condition is false and the wait happens as
before.

HONEST RESIDUAL, stated because this patch does not claim it: whether a fresh
`vkCreateDevice` succeeds after an NVIDIA Xid 109 - i.e. whether `handleDeviceLost`
can actually RECOVER rather than merely fail cleanly - is a separate question this
patch does not answer and could not answer without provoking a device loss
deliberately. What it establishes is the floor: a device loss ends in a clean throw
or a working recreate, never in an abort. The Xid 109 class itself is lane XID-2's.

SOURCE patch: every tree must re-run irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh. One file,
touched by no other patch in the stack.
---
diff --git a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/Vao/OgreVulkanStagingBuffer.cpp b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/Vao/OgreVulkanStagingBuffer.cpp
index 461b68e..7d204b6 100644
--- a/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/Vao/OgreVulkanStagingBuffer.cpp
+++ b/RenderSystems/Vulkan/src/Vao/OgreVulkanStagingBuffer.cpp
@@ -32,6 +32,7 @@ THE SOFTWARE.
 #include "Vao/OgreVulkanDynamicBuffer.h"
 #include "Vao/OgreVulkanVaoManager.h"
 
+#include "OgreLogManager.h"
 #include "OgreStringConverter.h"
 
 #include "OgreVulkanDevice.h"
@@ -57,15 +58,35 @@ namespace Ogre
     //-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     VulkanStagingBuffer::~VulkanStagingBuffer()
     {
-        if( !mFences.empty() )
+        VulkanVaoManager *vaoManager = static_cast<VulkanVaoManager *>( mVaoManager );
+
+        // Jahshaka (ogre-patch 0069): DO NOT WAIT ON A LOST DEVICE FROM A DESTRUCTOR.
+        //
+        // wait() ends in checkVkResult(), which THROWS on VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST - and a
+        // throw leaving a destructor (implicitly noexcept since C++11) is
+        // std::terminate. handleDeviceLost() destroys every staging buffer
+        // (destroyVkResources -> VaoManager::deleteStagingBuffers), so at this pin every
+        // device loss that reaches the recovery path ABORTS THE PROCESS instead of
+        // recovering or throwing cleanly - by construction, not by luck.
+        //
+        // A fence on a lost device will never be signalled and the wait is meaningless;
+        // vkDestroyFence and the pool return below are both legal on a lost device, so
+        // skipping only the wait is the whole fix.
+        if( !mFences.empty() && !vaoManager->getDevice()->isDeviceLost() )
             wait( mFences.back().fenceName );
+        else if( !mFences.empty() )
+        {
+            LogManager::getSingleton().logMessage(
+                "Vulkan: [INFO] Device is lost; not waiting on a staging buffer's fence "
+                "(it can never be signalled). Destroying it anyway.",
+                LML_CRITICAL );
+        }
 
         deleteFences( mFences.begin(), mFences.end() );
 
         // Staging Buffers are already delayed (see mZeroRefStagingBuffers) so they can
         // be deallocated immediately.
         // BT_DYNAMIC_DEFAULT already signals this information so no extra info is required.
-        VulkanVaoManager *vaoManager = static_cast<VulkanVaoManager *>( mVaoManager );
         vaoManager->deallocateVbo( mVboPoolIdx, mInternalBufferStart, getMaxSize(), BT_DYNAMIC_DEFAULT,
                                    !mUploadOnly, true );
     }
